  "text": "Allergies:\nShellfish / fish derived\n\nChief Complaint:\nSuicidal Ideation with Plan\n\nHistory of Present Illness:\n___ yo female to male transgender patient with prior diagnoses of \nBPAD, PTSD, prior suicide attempts, self-reported/diagnosed \nfibromyalgia was brought in by his friend/surrogate father for \nworsening depression, being off his psychiatric medications x \nweek and for SI with a plan to overdose on Lithium. \n\nPer pt, he was getting more physically unstable with \n\"fibromyalgia\" which caused him to leave his job at ___ \nwhen he was fainting. He also was supposed to get treatment for \nunderlying medical condition but rheumatologist and PCP wanted \nto coordinate with pt's psychiatrist, while pt decided to fire \nhiscurrent psychiatrist and doesn't have another contact yet. Pt \nstated he has been feeling depressed with anhedonia, decreased \nsleep, fatigue, and while he admits having suicidal ideation at \nbaseline he now developed a plan to overdose on ___ as well as \nconsidering other medications. He has ___ bottle and multiple \nother psychiatric medications stored at home. During the \ninteriview pt reported current intent to do so and not feeling \nsafe at home. He also admitted having thoughts about hanging \nhimself and stepping off a subway platform but cites overdose as \nhis actual \"means of choice\". \n\nPt stated he stopped psychiatric medications becuase he was \ndeveloping side effects. He is supposed to be on ___ but was not \nable to tolerate it as he developed nausea and vomiting, so he \nstopped it a week ago. He also stopped his testosterone because \nhe was unable to tolerate the injections. \n\nCollateral from ___, patient's friend/surrogate father, ph. \n___. \n___ stated that yesterday he went to pt's apartment to help \nwith cleaning, and found a key to his previous job which pt left \nthis ___. This per ___ triggered pt. Then ___ found \nout pt was not taking his psychiatric medications and had \nmultiple medications from prior trials stored at home. He then \ngot concerned when pt endorsed SI with a plan to OD on ___ while\nhaving a bottle of liquid ___ at home. ___ believes a \nsupervised setting would be beneficial for pt's safety and \npsychopharmacological treatment.\n\nBrief Hospital Course:\n----Psychiatric\n\n#Suicide: Upon admission the patient was resistant to treatment \nand noted ambivalence regarding death. The patient noted he has \nhas at least 15 suicide attempts in his life and at least four \n___ hospital stays for such. He reports that for him \nsuicidal thoughts are \"normal.\" The patient ascribed his suicide \nattempt to \"pain\" and fatigue of what he termed \"fibormyalgia\" \nand felt that unless such was addressed \"nothing will be \ndifferent.\" The team very carefully explained to the patient \nthat first he does not carry the diagnosis of fibromyalgia and \nsecond that even if he did, the first line treatment for such is \nadressing underlying mood disorders. We educated the patient \nregarding the somatic symtpoms of depression and how addressing \nhis mood disorder will help with such. The patient remained \nobstinate, refusing to partake in care or attend groups. The \npatient told the team on numerous occasions \"I do not know what \nyou want me to change\" and often stormed out of team meetings \nafter becoming agitated. Thus the team filed for section ___ and \nat court on ___, a judge agreed with the team that the \npatient was not safe to dicharge and he was comitted for up to \nthirty days. Even after court the patient was initially also \nresistant to work with the team or address his safety risk but \nover the next week the patient did begin to attend groups and \nwas noted to become a more frequent contributor. He was \neventually able to discuss with the team how he used suicide \nattempts as an escape and coping mechanism when living in \n___ with his family and how he understands such is not a \nhealthy or useful coping skill. He was able to work through \nseveral DBT exercises with the team and able to identify new \nskills and mechanisms he can use to reduce his risks for self \nharm. He was also noted to be future oriented, no longer \ndemanding to leave so he could \"go to sleep in my own bed\" but \ninstead talked about parties he hopes to attend with friend, \nplans for starting college, possible careers in ___, as \nwell as his interest in photopgraphy and art. He expressed \nunderstanding that he must seek immediate help if suicidal \nthoughts occur and agreed to such while also noting he did \nexactly that on this admission to demonstrate he knows when it \nis appropriate to seek help. He also agreed to continue working \nwith his outpatient treaters. Of note the patient does report \nsuicidal thoughts seem to be a chronic part of his life which we \ninformed him places him at high risk for self harm and/or \nsuicide but there are no new acute triggers or life events that \nplace him at an elevated risk beyond his baseline and now having \nlearned new coping skills and mechansims, he thinks he will be \nbetter able to handle his chronic risk. He noted that he plans \nto immediatly call his outpatient team or have a friend bring \nhim to the hospital if he either feels depressed, which he \nreports is a trigger for SI for him, or has SI. We referred him \nto a partial program and also ensured he has close follow up \nwith his treaters upon discharge.\n\n#Bipolar, current episode depressed: The patient noted that he \nself-dicontinued lithium due to what he termed \"stomach\" side \neffects and agreed to try seroquel here in the hospital. The \ndose of seroquel was increased during his time on the unit to a \ncurrent dose of 300mg which he tolerated well although did note \nsome fatigue on the first two days that resolved and after such \nnoted seroqel actually provides him with \"more energy\". He also \nfelt that seroquel helped with his depressive symptoms but was \nunable to explain exactly how. While on the unit he was never \nobserved to demonstrate manic signs or symptoms but rather was \nnoted to initially display some signs and symptoms of \ndepression, especially somatic complaints of pain and low \nenergy/fatigue. These somatic concerns lessened, but did not \nfully resolve during his time here on the unit. Throughout his \nstay on the unit, concern also arose the patient's presentation \nand history could also be well explained by a diagnosis of \nborderline personality disorder and while he certainly does meet \ncriterion for bipolar we feel that at least much of his current \nadmission can be ascribed to borderline personality which we \ndiagnosed and treated as outlined below. We also ensured the \npatient has follow up with his outpaient psychiatrist scheduled \nsoon after discharge.\n\n#Borderline Personality Disorder: The patient was never \ndiagnosed with such before this hospital stay but his \npresentation and history are consistant with such a diagnosis. \nHis outpatient psychiatrist, as well as the patient himself, \nagreed that such a diagnosis \"fits\" the patient when he \ncarefully explained what such was to the patient. We began DBT \nexercises with the patient designed around a series of skill \nbuilding lessons that we worked individually with the patient on \nover the course of his last week on the unit. While the patient \nclaimed DBT was \"not new\" to him, he was able to identify many \nnew coping skills and mechanisms that he could not previously \nidentify upon admission to the unit. We also notified his \ntherapist of the importance of continuing DBT work upon \ndischarge and the patiet agrees to keep developing his DBT \nskills.\n\n----Medical\n\nNo acute issues. The patient initially told the treatment team \nhe had firbomyalgia but later admitted such was not a true \ndiagnosis. He remaiend resistant to ascribing his pain and \nfatigue to untreated depression. We continued his home \nmedication of tramadol 50mg BID prn pain as he reported some \nresponse to such although we continued to educate him that his \npain will better respond to treating his mood disorder, \nexercise, and therapy.\n\n----Psychosocial\n\n#Groups/Milieu\n\nThe patient was noted to initially not attend groups stating \nthat \"such are a waste of time and make me upset.\" For nearly \nthe first two weeks of his hospital stay he was largely confined \nto his room and was not interactive with patients on the unit. \nAfter court he did become more social on the unit and was often \nnoted conversing, at times even smiling and laughing with other \npatients, and attending more groups. Of note though he denied \nand active suicidal thoughts or plans on the unit and no acute \nsafey issues were raised while he was here. \n\n#Family Meeting / Discussions\n\n-The patient refused to allow us to contact his family.\n\n-On ___, the team and patient did meet with the friend who \nbrought the patient into the hospital, ___: \n___ noted he housed the patient for 8 months here in ___ \nduring which he noticed the patient had few friends but was able \nto hold a job at a ___ ___ until a few weeks prior months \nago. He notes the patient has frequent suicidal thoughts and \nrecently has been restricted\nto his bed and unable to complete his activities of daily \nliving. The patient agreed with his friend that he feel unable \nto hold a job or say do laundry now given that he feels \n\"terrible.\" ___ confirmed that the patient stopped taking his \nLithium and was \"saving it up\" with apparent plan to overdose. \n___ is very\nconcerned for the safety of the patient and feels that if the \npatient were to leave the hospital he would either \"end up back \nin the ED or maybe kill himself.\" He also notes that perhaps \nevery two or three months the patient has periods were he is\n\"very active\" and needs little sleep, picks up new hobbies like \n\"soap making last time,\" and seems to talk more rapidly.  \n\n#Collateral\n\n-Outpatient psychiatrist Dr. ___ at ___: ___ \ncalled the team and confirmed the patient's history of numerous \nsuicide attempts via hanging and overdoses. Dr. ___ he is \nconcerned about the patient's safety given his plan to overdose \non Lithium as he explains that he took extra efforts to make the \npatient aware of the dangers of any overdose on such medication. \nHe also is concerned about the patient's history of poor follow \nup and missing several scheduled appointments. Further, he notes \nthe patient utilizes emergency room services \"several times per \nmonth\" although says not all are for psychiatric reasons. He \nsupports the teams effort to obtain a section ___.\n\n----Legal Status: CV accepted on ___. Three Day Noticed \nFiled on\n___. Section ___ filed on ___. Court granted section ___ \non ___ for up to 30 day commitment.\n\n----Risk Assessment\n\nThis patient has a low risk of imminent danger to himself and a \nhigh risk of chronic danger to himself. The patient has numerous \nrisk factors for suicide and self harm including at least 15 \nprior suicide attempts, numerous instances of self injurious \nbehavior, at times endorsing \"suicidal thoughts are normal,\" a \nhistory of medication and treatment non-complaince, limited \nsocial support, a reported history of chilhood trauma, a history \nof implusvity, a history of binge drinking, a history substance \nabuse, a reported hx of bipolar, and a new diagnosis of \nborderline personality disorder. \n\nStill he does have some protective factors including a history \nof at times help seeking behaviors, no access to firearms, \nfuture orientation with immediate goals to attend a party and \nget a job and long term goals of one day graduating college and \nworking in ___. While in the hospital we sought to \naddress his numerous risk factors. We restarted him on \nmedication for bipolar disorder, namely seroquel, which he \ntolerated well and noted improvement in his mood and that such \ngave him more energy. We addressed his concerns about pain and \nfatigue by explaining such were somatic symptoms of depression \nthat will improve with treatment and educated him about \nfibromyalgia and how he does not carry that diagnosis at this \ntime. We also held a meeting with his closest support, ___, to \nensure his support network is up to date and aware of his \ncondition. We also addressed his newly diagnosed borderline \npersonality disorder by beginning personal DBT skill sessions \nwith and having the patient work thought numerous DBT exercises \nin order to identifty and build new coping skills. through such \nexercises he was able to develop new awareness of his condition. \nWe also ensured he attended numerous group therapy sessions to \nlearn further coping skills and new ways to address his suicidal \nthoughts. We also ensured the patient has closely sceduled \nfollow up with his outpatient PCP, ___, and psychiatirst. \nFurther, we suggested and referred the patient to a partial \nprogram so that he may continue to build on the skills and \nimprovement he had made here. \n\nStill, given his chronic risk factors of numerous previous \nsuicide attempts, history of medication and treatment \nnon-complaince, reported hx of bipolar, and newly diagnosed \nborderline personality disorder, the patient remains at a very \nhigh risk for chronic self harm which we explained to both the \npatient and his support network. Of note he does understand the \nimportance of seeking help if he feels unsafe for any reason and \non numerous occassions told the team he will quickly seek aid if \nnecessary.\n\nDischarge Diagnosis:\nBorderline Personality Disorder, Bipolar Disorder",
